I've adapted @bramus [work](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9936#issuecomment-2682028665) (which was based on @Link2Twenty [work](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9075#issuecomment-2663527036)) to create something which works in React and TypeScript

<details><summary>Code</summary>
<p>

To use, change the value of PORTAL_CONTAINER_ID & the element in createPortal. I'm using this with a toast/sonner notification which **ISN'T** using the native popover API, you might need to adapt it further for your case if you are.

```tsx
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";
import { createPortal } from "react-dom";

type TopLayerChangedEvent = CustomEvent<{
  action: "add" | "remove";
  target: Element;
}>;

const PORTAL_CONTAINER_ID = "unique-id"; // Unique ID for DOM element + portal ID

// This wrapper moves the container to the top-most dialog/popover when one is opened, and back to the body when all are closed.
// I really wish this wasn't necessary but it currently is. Look at https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9936
// to see if this problem has been solved properly in the future.
const PopoverPortal = () => {
  // Adapted from https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9936#issuecomment-2682028665 which was based on https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9075#issuecomment-2663527036
  // and I (@LambArchie) have updated it to work with React

  // For clarity, this container should NOT re-render unless absolutely necessary, since doing so loses the state within it's children*
  // Therefore, this manipulates the DOM directly outside of React so it should only render twice
  // (once on mount which creates the default container, second when this created container is marked ready)
  // * If you are happy to lose the state of children, this code can be simplified by using useState to track the top-most element and getting rid of the portal container
  const containerRef = useRef<HTMLElement | null>(null);
  const [containerReady, setContainerReady] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Setup on mount - creates the portal container element if it doesn't exist (which it shouldn't on first render)
    // This element is not managed by React so moving it around won't cause re-renders
    let container = document.getElementById(PORTAL_CONTAINER_ID);
    if (!container) {
      container = document.createElement("div");
      container.id = PORTAL_CONTAINER_ID;
      document.body.appendChild(container);
    }
    containerRef.current = container;
    setContainerReady(true);

    // Store a set of top-layer elements so we can track them and know which is the top-most
    const topLayerElements = new Set<Element>();
    const getTopMostTopLayerElement = () => {
      const topLayers = Array.from(topLayerElements).filter(
        (el) => el instanceof HTMLElement,
      ) as HTMLElement[];
      // Not certain this will always be correct but from testing it seems to be fine under normal usage
      return topLayers.length > 0 ? topLayers[topLayers.length - 1] : null;
    };

    // Listen for toggle events which should be fired when a dialog or popover is toggled as top-layer
    const toggleListener = ({ target }: Event) => {
      if (!(target && target instanceof Element)) return;
      if (!(target instanceof HTMLDialogElement || target.hasAttribute("popover"))) {
        // Potentially add logging here in case another component gains the ability to make itself top-level.
        // Exclude <details> if you do, since that triggers this event too but isn't relevant
        return;
      }

      if (target.matches(":modal, :popover-open") && document.contains(target)) {
        topLayerElements.add(target);
        document.dispatchEvent(
          new CustomEvent("toplayerchanged", {
            detail: { action: "add", target },
          }) as TopLayerChangedEvent,
        );
      } else {
        // Only send event if the element was already tracked, as otherwise it doesn't matter
        if (topLayerElements.delete(target)) {
          document.dispatchEvent(
            new CustomEvent("toplayerchanged", {
              detail: { action: "remove", target },
            }) as TopLayerChangedEvent,
          );
        }
      }
    };
    document.addEventListener("toggle", toggleListener, { capture: true });

    // Detect when elements tracked as top-layer are removed from the DOM so we can move the container
    const observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
      const nodes = mutations.flatMap(({ removedNodes }) => Array.from(removedNodes));
      for (const node of nodes)
        if (node instanceof Element && topLayerElements.delete(node)) {
          document.dispatchEvent(
            new CustomEvent("toplayerchanged", {
              detail: { action: "remove", target: node },
            }) as TopLayerChangedEvent,
          );
        }
    });
    observer.observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true });

    // Handler to move the portal container in the DOM around
    const handleTopLayerChanged = (e: CustomEvent) => {
      const target = e.detail && e.detail.target;
      const action = e.detail && e.detail.action;
      if (
        action === "add" &&
        target &&
        target instanceof HTMLElement &&
        (target.nodeName.toLowerCase() === "dialog" || target.hasAttribute("popover"))
      ) {
        // Always append the container so it should hopefully be the last child on it.
        // Think this should be fine but might need to make the first child due to some edgecases
        // I've not encountered yet as it was in the original code I adapted this from.
        // appendChild also moves the element if it already exists in the DOM, which is great for this scenario
        target.appendChild(container);
      } else if (action === "remove") {
        // On remove, check for the next top-most dialog or popover
        const nextTopLayer = getTopMostTopLayerElement();
        if (nextTopLayer) {
          nextTopLayer.appendChild(container);
        } else {
          // Move the container back to the body if no top-layer elements are present
          document.body.appendChild(container);
        }
      }
    };
    document.addEventListener("toplayerchanged", handleTopLayerChanged as EventListener);

    return () => {
      document.removeEventListener("toggle", toggleListener, { capture: true });
      document.removeEventListener("toplayerchanged", handleTopLayerChanged as EventListener);
      observer.disconnect();
    };
  }, []);

  if (!containerReady || !containerRef.current) return null;

  return createPortal(
    <Popover />, // CHANGE THIS TO YOUR COMPONENT
    containerRef.current,
    PORTAL_CONTAINER_ID, // Think this is unnecessary since this isn't re-rendered but have it just in case
  );
};

export default PopoverPortal;
```

</p>
</details>

@ivands There's currently no workaround. You have to make the popover a child of the modal to make it interactive.

You _could_ use the `toggle` event emitted by modals to remount popover elements as children of the top-most modal. Example from earlier in this thread <https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9936#issuecomment-2682028665>


But this is tricky to get right when using a framework like `react` that doesn't cope well when the DOM changes independently of its internal representation. And the moved components themselves may break in unexpected ways.
